1
00:00:00,005 --> 00:00:02,505
(chill music)

2
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:08,130
- [Cristian] Hello everyone.

3
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:10,800
This is Professor Cristian Lopez Vargas,

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,660
and in this video we're going to discuss

5
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:14,220
what is microeconomics

6
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:19,220
and what is the structure of this course.

7
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:21,210
So, what is microeconomics?

8
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:26,210
Microeconomics is mainly the
study of individual behavior.

9
00:00:26,790 --> 00:00:29,280
Individual behavior can
refer to one person,

10
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,020
for example, one consumer,

11
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:34,860
or it can refer to the
behavior of one firm,

12
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,363
one company, for example.

13
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:42,560
Microeconomics also studies
how consumers aggregate

14
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:46,803
or form together what
we call the demand side,

15
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:50,790
and similarly, microeconomics also studies

16
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:54,090
how firms or companies together form

17
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:56,223
what we call the supply side.

18
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:01,620
The supply side and the
demand side come together

19
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:04,470
and operate in what we call a market.

20
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:06,990
And markets can take different structures,

21
00:01:06,990 --> 00:01:09,210
and shapes, and features,

22
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:13,560
and there are markets
that are more competitive.

23
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:14,793
There are some types of markets

24
00:01:14,793 --> 00:01:18,300
that we call perfectly competitive.

25
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:19,860
And there are other types of markets

26
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:24,000
that are less competitive
or more monopolistic.

27
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,790
In that other extreme we
have monopolies, of course,

28
00:01:26,790 --> 00:01:30,660
where the market is
served by only one agent,

29
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:32,733
and there are other markets that are,

30
00:01:34,635 --> 00:01:36,930
that operate under an oligopoly,

31
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:40,053
which means that fewer firms
are serving the market.

32
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:44,970
Microeconomics also studies
what we call game theory.

33
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:49,860
Game theory is a study of
situations that we call strategic.

34
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:52,428
Strategic situations are those situations

35
00:01:52,428 --> 00:01:57,428
in which what my opponent or
my competitor does affects me,

36
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:02,580
and therefore, when I decide
to choose my best action,

37
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:05,760
I need to consider what the other person,

38
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:08,970
or the other opponent,
or the competitor firm

39
00:02:08,970 --> 00:02:10,770
will do as well.

40
00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:14,130
Finally, microeconomics also studies

41
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:16,650
what we call market failures.

42
00:02:16,650 --> 00:02:18,150
Market failures arise

43
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:22,140
when information is
asymmetrically distributed

44
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:23,970
among economic agents,

45
00:02:23,970 --> 00:02:28,893
or when there exists something
that we call externalities.

46
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:33,783
Let us briefly discuss what is
the structure of this course.

47
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:37,863
First, we will discuss what
we call consumer theory.

48
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:42,930
Consumer theory is everything
related to the consumer,

49
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:47,930
meaning how his preferences
and constraints come together

50
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:53,280
and form what we call the optimal bundle.

51
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:56,160
And how, collectively, consumers aggregate

52
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:59,310
into what we call the demand side.

53
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:03,360
Then we will discuss what
we call producer theory.

54
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,990
Producer theory is everything
related to the firm,

55
00:03:06,990 --> 00:03:11,250
including its cost, its technology,

56
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:14,280
how the firm finds the optimal bundle,

57
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:17,640
and how multiple firms aggregate

58
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:19,683
into what we call the supply side.

59
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,640
Then we will discuss markets.

60
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:28,640
Markets can be competitive,
which happens when,

61
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:32,460
for example, we have many
firms, and many consumers,

62
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:35,463
and information flows freely.

63
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:40,320
And markets can also have other structures

64
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:43,800
in which there is market power.

65
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,530
Market power is what we call,

66
00:03:46,530 --> 00:03:50,280
is a situation where
one firm, for example,

67
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:54,780
has power of one side of the market.

68
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:59,250
One simple example is a monopoly.

69
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:03,240
In a monopoly, only one
firm serves the whole market

70
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:07,203
and therefore has market
power because of that.

71
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,250
Finally, we will discuss game theory,

72
00:04:11,250 --> 00:04:13,200
and as I mentioned before,

73
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:18,125
game theory is the study
of strategic interactions.

74
00:04:18,125 --> 00:04:20,625
(chill music)

